From 3db9e9fe1745a26144f0ec0578faf8a922a4ab61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:13:13 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] COPY/CONVERT: import host text as VAR records with RAT:CR
 (#15)

VMS's native text format is variable-length records with CR carriage
control - what VMS FTP writes in ASCII mode, and what some older
utilities and kit procedures expect - but COPY could only make stream
formats, refusing RFM:VAR since a host file has no record counts.

COPY/CONVERT now converts a host text file into VAR records, one per
line:

    COPY/CONVERT notes.txt [DOC]NOTES.TXT
    COPY/ATTRIBUTES=(RFM:VAR,RAT:CR)/CONVERT notes.txt [DOC]NOTES.TXT

Lines end at LF, a CR just before the LF is dropped, and a last line
with no LF is still a record. Each record is a 2-byte little-endian
count, the bytes, and a pad byte to an even length; records run on
across blocks and end at the EOF mark, with no marker after them.
FAT$W_RSIZE is the longest line; MRS is 0 unless given; RAT defaults to
CR. A line over 32,767 bytes is refused, naming the line. RAT:BLK, LRL,
an MRS below the longest line, or /CONVERT with another RFM are
refused. RFM:VAR without /CONVERT says to use it.

The new src/ods2_text_var.c does it in two passes, building on #16's
streaming: a scan gives the converted length and longest record before
anything is allocated, then an encoder produces the records a chunk at
a time as COPY writes them, so memory stays flat. A pipe is spooled to
a temporary file first, as for any COPY. ods2_content_source_t gains
var_records, and ods2_check_var_record_attrs() checks such content;
VAR content not marked as converted is still refused.

Tests: ods2_text_var_selftest checks the scan and encoder agree and
produce exactly the expected records (LF, CRLF, empty lines, no final
LF, lone CRs, odd/even lengths, empty file, the 32767-byte limit) read
in awkward piece sizes, the refusals, and end to end on a volume: VAR,
RAT:CR, LRL, MRS 0, EOF, records decoding back to the lines.
cli_record_attrs_check.sh covers the CLI forms, a pipe, an empty file,
and each refusal.

Not yet checked on VMS: DIRECTORY/FULL ("Variable length ... Carriage
return carriage control") and TYPE of a converted file.

